VERDICT
Correct: your code correctly uses a base-26 multiplication to calculate the column number, though the hash map is not needed.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well: You correctly identified that Excel column titles operate on a Base-26 number system. You set up the loop and the multiplication step perfectly.

Is it correct? Yes, it is fully correct and will pass all test cases.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses: You do not need an unordered_map to associate letters with numbers. Because letters are stored sequentially in standard ASCII, you can use pure arithmetic to extract the value. Also, comparing a signed integer i to columnTitle.length() (which returns an unsigned size_t) can trigger compiler warnings.

Time and space complexity of MY code: Time complexity is O(n) where n is the length of the string, because you look at each letter once. Space complexity is O(1) auxiliary space because your map always holds exactly 26 items no matter how long the input string is.

Why my approach works: It works like our normal decimal numbers, but the base is 26 instead of 10. For an input like "AB", it evaluates to 1 times 26 plus 2, giving the correct answer of 28.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED

Approach 1: Hash Map and Loop (Your Approach)

Where it comes from: Common beginner method for translating symbols to numbers.

Idea: Build a dictionary that pairs 'A' with 1, 'B' with 2, all the way to 'Z' with 26. Read the string left to right, multiplying the running total by 26 and adding the dictionary value of the current letter.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int titleToNumber(string columnTitle) {
        unordered_map<char, int> store;
        int j = 1;
        for (char i = 'A'; i <= 'Z'; i++) {
            store[i] = j;
            j++;
        }
        int ans = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < columnTitle.length(); i++) {
            ans = ans * 26 + store[columnTitle[i]];
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
Time and space complexity: Time is O(n). Space is O(1).

What makes the next approach better: Building a hash table wastes a tiny bit of time and memory. We can skip it by subtracting letters from each other because of how computers store text as numbers.

Approach 2: Direct ASCII Math (Optimal)

Where it comes from: Official LeetCode editorial and standard text processing tricks.

Idea: In a computer, 'A' is stored as a number (65). If you take any letter and subtract 'A', you get its distance from the start. Add 1 to that distance, and you get the exact number value you need without building a map.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int titleToNumber(string columnTitle) {
        int ans = 0;
        for (char c : columnTitle) {
            ans = ans * 26 + (c - 'A' + 1);
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
Time and space complexity: Time is O(n) because it checks each letter once. Space is O(1) because it only uses one integer variable.

What makes the next approach better: This is the best possible approach. It is fast, uses practically no memory, and is very short.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use? You used Approach 1.

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem? An interviewer expects Approach 2. They want to see that you understand how characters are stored as numbers and that you can avoid heavy data structures like hash maps when simple math works.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:
Question: What if the string is extremely long, like 15 characters?
Answer: The final answer would grow too large and overflow a standard 32-bit integer. We would need to change the answer variable to a 64-bit integer, like long long in C++.
Question: Can you do the reverse operation, turning a number back into a column title?
Answer: Yes. We can use a while loop, repeatedly taking the number modulo 26 to get the last letter, and then dividing the number by 26 to shrink it, which builds the string backwards.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence: Excel columns are a Base-26 number system, and you can find a letter's value using pure arithmetic like c - 'A' + 1.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time: Skip the hash map when working with simple alphabet values. Use a modern range-based loop to avoid bounds and type signedness warnings.

The pattern or idea this question is really about: Base conversions and ASCII character arithmetic.

